WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.910
A student brought me a project with

2
00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.010
ninety nine percent accuracy.

3
00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:09.550
I asked one question: accuracy on what?

4
00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.190
It was the same data the

5
00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:13.310
model was trained on.

6
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.580
On new data, it fell to sixty one.

7
00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.120
Today: the bias–variance trade-off, honest

8
00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.120
error estimates with cross-validation, and

9
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.220
the leakage mistakes students make.

10
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.640
For squared error, the expected error

11
00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.660
on a new point splits into three

12
00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:36.100
parts: bias squared, variance, and

13
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:37.700
noise we can never remove.

14
00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:40.530
Simple models have high bias.

15
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.960
Flexible models have high variance.

16
00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.960
As complexity grows, training error

17
00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:50.880
keeps falling, but test error falls

18
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.740
and then rises again.

19
00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.120
The best model sits at the bottom of that

20
00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.480
U, not at the lowest training error.

21
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.360
We fitted polynomials of degree one to

22
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.160
fifteen to two hundred points.

23
00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:07.940
Test error was lowest at degree four.

24
00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:10.750
By degree fifteen the curve

25
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:12.510
wiggled through every training

26
00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:14.370
point and missed new ones.

27
00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:17.610
Cross-validation estimates test

28
00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.030
error without wasting data.

29
00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:22.990
Split the data into k folds, train on

30
00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:25.550
k minus one, test on the one left

31
00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:27.630
out, and average over all k.

32
00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:30.610
Five or ten folds is the usual choice.

33
00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:35.170
The commonest student bug is leakage.

34
00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:40.070
They scale or select features on

35
00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:42.310
all the data, then split.

36
00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:45.350
The test fold has already

37
00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:46.630
shaped the model.

38
00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:50.180
Every preprocessing step must be

39
00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:53.000
fitted inside each training fold.

40
00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:54.700
Quick check!

41
00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:58.180
Model A has training error two

42
00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:00.440
and test error twenty.

43
00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:04.100
Model B has training error twelve

44
00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:05.940
and test error fourteen.

45
00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:08.480
Which should we choose, and why?

46
00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:10.530
Model B.

47
00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:14.490
The gap in model A between two and twenty

48
00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:17.030
is the signature of high variance.

49
00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:19.380
We judge models on data

50
00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:20.600
they have not seen.

51
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.300
In the app, open the overfitting lab.

52
00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:27.160
Raise the model's complexity step by

53
00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:29.320
step and watch training error fall

54
00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:31.380
while test error turns back up.

55
00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:33.290
Your turn.

56
00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:37.950
A student standardises all the data, then

57
00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.590
runs five-fold cross-validation.

58
00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:43.010
Is the estimate honest?

59
00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:45.730
No, it leaks.

60
00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:50.290
Remember: error is bias plus variance plus

61
00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:53.810
noise, choose the bottom of the U, and fit

62
00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:55.970
every step inside the training fold.

63
00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:59.560
Try it in your next lecture: hand

64
00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:01.960
out two short notebooks, one with

65
00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:03.460
leakage and one without.

66
00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:06.480
Then ask pairs to find why their

67
00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.200
cross-validation scores differ before you

68
00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:09.500
explain.
